        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">San people</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: recent ancestral territories across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, Lesotho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, many communities in the Kalahari; about 63,500 San in Botswana (2017) and 71,201 in Namibia (2023); places include the Tsodilo Hills, Nyae Nyae, Ghanzi and the Central Kalahari Game Reserve.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hunter-gatherer groups spread from Tanzania to Cape Agulhas some 10,000 years ago; the Tshua and Khwe San in the first millennium BCE; most San leave the coasts after the Khoe and Xhosa migrations from 650 CE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlement at the Cape from 1652 and frontier killings through the 18th century; government farming programmes from the 1950s to the 1990s.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">hunting with bows and poisoned arrows and long tracking; gathering by women; sip <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and ostrich-egg water containers; rock paintings across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; rainmaking and trance; a gift economy and decisions by consensus; languages with click consonants; some of the earliest-branching human genetic lineages</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">San oral tradition and mythology, recorded by Wilhelm Bleek and Lucy Lloyd (Specimens of Bushman Folklore, 1911) and Dorothea Bleek; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and colonial records; archaeology such as Border Cave; the ethnography and films of Lorna Marshall, John Marshall and Elizabeth Marshall Thomas; rock-art studies by David Lewis-Williams; Y-chromosome, mitochondrial and whole-genome studies; San councils and organisations today.</span></div>
        </div>
