    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the San</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope.jpg" alt="Cape of Good Hope" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cape of Good Hope <span class="kid-tale-cr">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 10,000 years ago, the ancestors of the San lived from Tanzania all the way to the south of Africa.</p><p>The San have no single name for themselves, but use their nations’ names, such as ǃKung, ǀXam and Khwe.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-cape.jpg" alt="Eastern Cape" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Eastern Cape <span class="kid-tale-cr">Felix Gottwald, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>For thousands of years they moved with the seasons, following water, animals and plants.</p><p>Men hunted with bows and poisoned arrows, women gathered fruit and roots, and people shared through gifts.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drakensberg.jpg" alt="Drakensberg" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Drakensberg <span class="kid-tale-cr">Diriye Amey from Locarno, Switzerland, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>San painters left pictures on rocks across southern Africa, showing hunting, daily life and stories.</p><p>San shamans, people who deal with the spirit world, went into a trance to bring rain.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trekboers.jpg" alt="Trekboers" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Trekboers <span class="kid-tale-cr">Samuel Daniell, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>From about 1714, Dutch stock farmers called Trekboers moved into San lands and took their waterholes.</p><p>Their armed groups killed thousands of San, and many others were forced to work on farms.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-people.jpg" alt="San people" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>San people <span class="kid-tale-cr">Velatrix, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today many San live in Botswana and Namibia, and they work together to defend their rights and their land.</p><p>In 2003 they won an agreement to be paid for their knowledge of a plant called hoodia.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The San, who call themselves by the names of their own nations such as the ǃKung, ǀXam and Khwe, descend from hunter-gatherer societies that some 10,000 years ago were spread from Tanzania to Cape Agulhas.</li><li>Traditionally semi-nomadic and egalitarian, they lived by hunting and gathering, took decisions by consensus with women as relative equals, and shared through a gift economy.</li><li>From the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonists’ commandos killed thousands of San at the Cape, and in 1777 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> endorsed a policy of eradicating them; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> the San have since been moved off land such as the Central Kalahari Game Reserve.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In long droughts the San dig into damp sand and suck water up through a hollow grass stem, letting it run down a second straw into an empty ostrich egg.</p></aside>
    </section>

